# Clean Code Rules

You are an expert software engineer.

Generate, refactor, and review code according to these rules.

## Core Objective

Write code that is:

* easy to read;
* easy to change;
* easy to test;
* hard to misunderstand.

Prefer clarity over cleverness.
Prefer explicit intent over comments.
Prefer small focused units over large generic abstractions.
Prefer maintainability over premature optimization.
Prefer boring, predictable code over impressive code.

Clean code is not code that looks smart.
Clean code is code that makes the next change safe.

---

# 1. Meaningful Names

Names are the first form of documentation.

A good name must answer:

* why it exists;
* what it does;
* how it is used.

If a name requires a comment to explain it, the name is not good enough.

## Good Practices

Use intention-revealing names.

Bad:

```ts
const d = 5;
```

Good:

```ts
const elapsedTimeInDays = 5;
```

Use pronounceable names.

Bad:

```ts
const genymdhms = new Date();
```

Good:

```ts
const generationTimestamp = new Date();
```

Use searchable names.

Bad:

```ts
if (student.classes.length > 7) {
  throw new Error("Too many classes");
}
```

Good:

```ts
const MAX_CLASSES_PER_STUDENT = 7;

if (student.classes.length > MAX_CLASSES_PER_STUDENT) {
  throw new Error("Too many classes");
}
```

Use longer names for wider scopes.

Bad:

```ts
const r = url.toLowerCase();
```

Good:

```ts
const lowercaseUrl = url.toLowerCase();
```

Very short names are acceptable only in tiny, obvious scopes.

Acceptable:

```ts
for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
  processItem(items[i]);
}
```

Avoid short names for important concepts.

Bad:

```ts
const u = await getUser();
const p = await getPermissions(u);
```

Good:

```ts
const user = await getUser();
const permissions = await getPermissions(user);
```

Use nouns or noun phrases for classes, objects, and types.

Good:

```ts
class Customer {}
class Account {}
class Payment {}
class Invoice {}
```

Avoid vague class names.

Bad:

```ts
class CustomerManager {}
class AccountData {}
class PaymentInfo {}
```

Use verbs or verb phrases for functions and methods.

Good:

```ts
postPayment();
deletePage();
saveUser();
calculateInvoiceTotal();
```

Use one word per concept.

Do not mix different verbs for the same operation unless they mean different things.

Bad:

```ts
fetchUser();
retrieveOrder();
getInvoice();
```

Good:

```ts
getUser();
getOrder();
getInvoice();
```

Use solution-domain names when appropriate.

Good:

```ts
class PaymentFactory {}
class UserRepository {}
class AuthenticationStrategy {}
class InvoiceMapper {}
```

Use problem-domain names when they are clearer.

Good:

```ts
class Subscription {}
class ShippingAddress {}
class LoyaltyDiscount {}
class RenewalPolicy {}
```

## Naming Code Smells

Avoid disinformation.

Do not use names that imply a wrong type, behavior, or abstraction.

Bad:

```ts
const accountList = new Set<Account>();
```

Good:

```ts
const accounts = new Set<Account>();
```

Avoid meaningless distinctions.

Bad:

```ts
const productInfo = getProductInfo();
const productData = getProductData();
```

Good:

```ts
const productDetails = getProductDetails();
const productInventory = getProductInventory();
```

Avoid numbered names.

Bad:

```ts
const a1 = getCustomer();
const a2 = getAdmin();
```

Good:

```ts
const customer = getCustomer();
const admin = getAdmin();
```

Avoid encodings and prefixes.

Bad:

```ts
const strName = "Mario";
const m_userAge = 35;
```

Good:

```ts
const name = "Mario";
const userAge = 35;
```

Avoid mental mapping.

Bad:

```ts
const r = url.toLowerCase();
```

Good:

```ts
const lowercaseUrl = url.toLowerCase();
```

Avoid cute, ironic, funny, or slang names.

Bad:

```ts
function holyHandGrenade(items: Item[]) {
  deleteItems(items);
}
```

Good:

```ts
function deleteItems(items: Item[]) {
  // ...
}
```

## Naming Blacklist

Avoid vague names unless the existing project convention requires them or there is a strong reason.

Suspicious names:

* `Manager`
* `Processor`
* `Handler`
* `Helper`
* `Util`
* `Common`
* `Data`
* `Info`
* `Object`
* `Thing`
* `Stuff`
* `Temp`
* `Foo`
* `Bar`
* `Doer`
* `Service` when it hides multiple responsibilities

Use a more specific name that describes the responsibility.

Bad:

```ts
class UserManager {}
```

Better:

```ts
class UserRegistration {}
class UserAuthenticator {}
class UserPermissionChecker {}
```

