# 2. Functions

Functions are the first line of organization in a program.

They must be small, focused, and written at one level of abstraction.

## Good Practices

Keep functions very small.

Rules:

* Prefer functions under 20 lines.
* If a function becomes hard to scan, split it.
* A function should rarely require scrolling.
* A function should be understandable from its name and body without extra explanation.

Do one thing.

A function should do one thing, do it well, and do it only.

If part of a function can be extracted into another function with a meaningful name that is not just a restatement of the implementation, the original function is probably doing more than one thing.

Bad:

```ts
function registerUser(user: User) {
  validateUser(user);
  database.users.insert(user);
  emailClient.send(user.email, "Welcome");
  auditLogger.log("User registered");
}
```

Good:

```ts
function registerUser(user: User) {
  validateUser(user);
  saveUser(user);
  sendWelcomeEmail(user);
  logUserRegistration(user);
}
```

Use one level of abstraction per function.

Bad:

```ts
function renderPage(page: Page) {
  const html = getPageHtml(page);
  response.write("<html>");
  response.write(html);
  response.write("</html>");
}
```

Good:

```ts
function renderPage(page: Page) {
  const html = buildPageHtml(page);
  sendHtmlResponse(html);
}
```

Follow the step-down rule.

Code should read top-down like a story:

1. high-level intent first;
2. lower-level implementation details below;
3. private helpers after the functions that call them.

Use few arguments.

Preferred order:

1. zero arguments;
2. one argument;
3. two arguments;
4. three arguments only when strongly justified;
5. more than three arguments almost never.

For many values, prefer an object parameter.

Bad:

```ts
createUser(name, email, age, country, role, isActive);
```

Good:

```ts
createUser({
  name,
  email,
  age,
  country,
  role,
  isActive,
});
```

Follow Command-Query Separation.

A function should either:

* perform an action;
* return information.

It should not do both.

Bad:

```ts
if (user.setActive(true)) {
  sendActivationEmail(user);
}
```

Good:

```ts
user.activate();

if (user.isActive()) {
  sendActivationEmail(user);
}
```

Use exceptions or typed errors instead of error codes.

Bad:

```ts
const result = saveUser(user);

if (result === -1) {
  showError();
}
```

Good:

```ts
try {
  saveUser(user);
} catch (error) {
  showSaveUserError(error);
}
```

Keep error handling separate from business logic.

A function that handles errors should do only error handling.

## Function Code Smells

Avoid side effects.

A function must not secretly do something that its name does not reveal.

Bad:

```ts
function checkPassword(user: User, password: string) {
  const isValid = passwordHasher.verify(password, user.passwordHash);

  if (isValid) {
    session.initialize(user);
  }

  return isValid;
}
```

Good:

```ts
function isPasswordValid(user: User, password: string) {
  return passwordHasher.verify(password, user.passwordHash);
}

function login(user: User, password: string) {
  if (!isPasswordValid(user, password)) {
    throw new InvalidCredentialsError();
  }

  session.initialize(user);
}
```

Avoid boolean flag arguments.

Boolean flags usually mean the function does more than one thing.

Bad:

```ts
renderPage(true);
```

Good:

```ts
renderPublicPage();
renderPrivatePage();
```

Avoid output arguments.

Readers expect arguments to be inputs, not values that will be mutated.

Bad:

```ts
function appendFooter(report: Report) {
  report.sections.push(createFooter());
}
```

Good:

```ts
function withFooter(report: Report) {
  return {
    ...report,
    sections: [...report.sections, createFooter()],
  };
}
```

Avoid duplication.

Duplicated logic is one of the main causes of bugs and design decay.

Bad:

```ts
function calculateStandardUserPrice(price: number) {
  return price - price * 0.1;
}

function calculatePremiumUserPrice(price: number) {
  return price - price * 0.1;
}
```

Good:

```ts
const LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.1;

function applyLoyaltyDiscount(price: number) {
  return price - price * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;
}
```

Avoid repeated switch or if/else chains.

If the same conditional logic appears in multiple places, use polymorphism, strategy objects, lookup maps, or a factory.

Bad:

```ts
function calculatePay(employee: Employee) {
  switch (employee.type) {
    case "commissioned":
      return calculateCommissionedPay(employee);
    case "hourly":
      return calculateHourlyPay(employee);
    case "salaried":
      return calculateSalariedPay(employee);
  }
}
```

Better:

```ts
interface Employee {
  calculatePay(): Money;
}

class CommissionedEmployee implements Employee {
  calculatePay() {
    return calculateCommissionedPay(this);
  }
}

class HourlyEmployee implements Employee {
  calculatePay() {
    return calculateHourlyPay(this);
  }
}

class SalariedEmployee implements Employee {
  calculatePay() {
    return calculateSalariedPay(this);
  }
}
```

A switch may be acceptable when it is isolated inside a factory, adapter, or boundary layer.

---

# 3. Comments

Comments are a last resort, not a substitute for clear code.

## Rules

First try to make the code explain itself.

Prefer:

* better names;
* extracted functions;
* clearer structure;
* stronger types;
* named constants;
* smaller units.

Bad:

```ts
if (employee.age > 65 && employee.yearsOfService > 10) {
  // Eligible for full benefits
}
```

Good:

```ts
if (employee.isEligibleForFullBenefits()) {
  // ...
}
```

Remove bad comments:

* redundant comments;
* comments that repeat the code;
* historical change logs;
* commented-out code;
* stale comments;
* misleading comments;
* speculative comments;
* noise comments.

Acceptable comments explain:

* why a non-obvious decision was made;
* external constraints;
* legal implications;
* security implications;
* warnings about dangerous behavior;
* public API contracts when required.

Do not write comments to compensate for bad names or messy structure.

Bad:

```ts
// Increment index
i++;
```

Bad:

```ts
// TODO: maybe refactor this someday
```

Better:

```ts
// Required by the external payment provider: retries must use exponential backoff.
```

