# 4. Formatting

Formatting is communication.

## Rules

Organize source files like a newspaper:

1. important concepts at the top;
2. high-level code first;
3. details and helpers lower down.

Declare local variables close to where they are used.

Keep related concepts vertically close.

Put calling functions above called functions when possible.

Separate unrelated concepts with blank lines.

Keep lines reasonably short.

* Prefer max 120 characters per line.

Use whitespace to improve readability.

Do not compress complex logic into dense one-liners.

Respect the project formatter.

Do not fight:

* Prettier;
* ESLint;
* Biome;
* Black;
* gofmt;
* rustfmt;
* the formatter already used by the project.

---

# 5. Classes, Objects, and Data Structures

Classes and modules must have one reason to change.

## Rules

Follow the Single Responsibility Principle.

A class, module, component, or service should have one clear responsibility.

Keep classes small.

Keep instance fields few and cohesive.

If only some methods use some fields, split the class.

Avoid feature envy.

If a method uses another object’s data more than its own, move the behavior closer to that data.

Bad:

```ts
function getCustomerCountryCode(order: Order) {
  return order.customer.address.country.code;
}
```

Good:

```ts
function getCustomerCountryCode(order: Order) {
  return order.customerCountryCode();
}
```

Follow the Law of Demeter.

Talk to direct collaborators, not deep object chains.

Bad:

```ts
order.customer.address.country.code;
```

Good:

```ts
order.customerCountryCode();
```

Do not create hybrids between objects and data structures.

Objects:

* hide data;
* expose behavior.

Data structures:

* expose data;
* contain little or no behavior.

Do not mix both styles in the same abstraction.

Prefer composition over large inheritance hierarchies.

Keep domain behavior inside domain objects when it clearly belongs there.

Avoid anemic domain models when behavior clearly belongs with the data.

---

# 6. Error Handling and Null

Error handling must not obscure the main logic.

## Rules

Prefer exceptions or typed error results over error codes.

Do not return magic error values:

* `-1`;
* `false`;
* `null`;
* `"ERROR"`;
* `E_OK`.

Do not return `null`.

Prefer:

* empty collections;
* Optional/Maybe;
* Result/Either;
* Special Case objects;
* exceptions.

Bad:

```ts
function getUsers(): User[] | null {
  return database.users.findMany() ?? null;
}
```

Good:

```ts
function getUsers(): User[] {
  return database.users.findMany() ?? [];
}
```

Do not pass `null`.

Use explicit optional types, overloads, or dedicated functions.

Bad:

```ts
createUser(name, null);
```

Good:

```ts
createUserWithoutReferral(name);
```

Validate inputs at boundaries.

Fail fast when required data is missing.

Keep try/catch blocks small.

Do not swallow errors silently.

Add context when rethrowing errors.

Bad:

```ts
try {
  await saveUser(user);
} catch {
  // ignore
}
```

Good:

```ts
try {
  await saveUser(user);
} catch (error) {
  throw new UserSaveError("Failed to save user", { cause: error });
}
```

---

# 7. Types and Contracts

Use types to make invalid states impossible.

## Rules

Prefer explicit domain types over primitive obsession.

Do not pass generic strings, numbers, or booleans when a domain type would be clearer.

Avoid:

* `any`;
* unsafe casts;
* broad `unknown` without narrowing;
* optional fields that are not truly optional;
* untyped external input.

Model states explicitly.

Prefer:

* discriminated unions;
* enums when appropriate;
* value objects;
* branded types;
* typed IDs;
* Result/Either types when exceptions are not suitable.

Validate external input at system boundaries.

After validation, internal code should rely on trusted types.

Bad:

```ts
function payInvoice(invoiceId: string, amount: number, currency: string) {
  // ...
}
```

Good:

```ts
function payInvoice(invoiceId: InvoiceId, payment: Money) {
  // ...
}
```

Bad:

```ts
type Order = {
  status: string;
};
```

Good:

```ts
type OrderStatus = "draft" | "confirmed" | "paid" | "cancelled";

type Order = {
  status: OrderStatus;
};
```

Prefer types that prevent invalid combinations.

Bad:

```ts
type RequestState = {
  loading: boolean;
  error?: Error;
  data?: User;
};
```

Good:

```ts
type RequestState =
  | { status: "idle" }
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "failed"; error: Error }
  | { status: "succeeded"; data: User };
```

---

# 8. Avoid Primitive Obsession

Do not represent important domain concepts with raw primitives.

Bad:

```ts
const email = "user@example.com";
const price = 100;
const currency = "EUR";
```

Good:

```ts
const email = EmailAddress.create("user@example.com");
const price = Money.eur(100);
```

## Rules

Wrap important domain values in named abstractions.

Do not pass related primitives around as loose parameters.

Group related values into meaningful objects.

Put validation close to the domain concept.

Bad:

```ts
function createSubscription(userId: string, planId: string, startDate: string) {
  // ...
}
```

Good:

```ts
function createSubscription(command: CreateSubscriptionCommand) {
  // ...
}
```

Better:

```ts
type CreateSubscriptionCommand = {
  userId: UserId;
  planId: PlanId;
  startDate: SubscriptionStartDate;
};
```

---

# 9. Boundaries and Architecture

Keep boundaries clear.

## Rules

Keep domain logic independent from frameworks.

Keep business rules independent from:

* HTTP;
* database;
* UI;
* queues;
* third-party APIs;
* file systems;
* environment variables;
* framework-specific objects.

Put I/O at the edges of the system.

Convert external data into internal domain types at boundaries.

Do not leak database models, API DTOs, or framework-specific objects into domain logic.

Do not let controllers, routes, jobs, or UI components contain complex business logic.

Bad:

```ts
app.post("/orders", async (req, res) => {
  const discount = req.body.total > 100 && req.user.tier === "gold" ? 0.1 : 0;
  const order = await database.orders.insert({
    total: req.body.total - req.body.total * discount,
  });

  res.json(order);
});
```

Good:

```ts
app.post("/orders", async (req, res) => {
  const command = createOrderCommandSchema.parse(req.body);
  const result = await createOrder(command);

  res.json(result);
});
```

Domain logic should live in domain services, application services, entities, policies, or pure functions.

Framework code should coordinate, not decide.

---

# 10. Testability

Write code that is easy to test.

## Rules

Keep business logic separate from:

* framework code;
* I/O;
* database access;
* network calls;
* UI rendering;
* system time;
* randomness;
* global state.

Prefer pure functions for calculations and domain logic.

Inject dependencies instead of creating them inside functions or classes.

Avoid hidden global state.

Avoid direct access to time, randomness, environment variables, and external APIs inside core logic unless abstracted.

When behavior changes, add or update tests.

Tests must verify behavior, not implementation details.

Test names should describe expected behavior.

Bad:

```ts
function calculateDiscount(order: Order) {
  const today = new Date();

  return isBlackFriday(today) ? order.total * 0.2 : 0;
}
```

Good:

```ts
function calculateDiscount(order: Order, today: Date) {
  return isBlackFriday(today) ? order.total * 0.2 : 0;
}
```

Bad:

```ts
class UserService {
  private emailClient = new EmailClient();

  async register(user: User) {
    await saveUser(user);
    await this.emailClient.sendWelcomeEmail(user.email);
  }
}
```

Good:

```ts
class UserService {
  constructor(private readonly emailClient: EmailClient) {}

  async register(user: User) {
    await saveUser(user);
    await this.emailClient.sendWelcomeEmail(user.email);
  }
}
```

---

# 11. Complexity Control

Keep control flow simple.

## Rules

Prefer early returns over deeply nested conditionals.

Avoid more than two levels of nesting.

Extract complex conditions into named predicates.

Replace repeated conditional logic with:

* polymorphism;
* strategies;
* lookup maps;
* factories;
* domain methods.

Avoid clever boolean expressions.

Prefer guard clauses for invalid or edge cases.

Bad:

```ts
if (user) {
  if (user.isActive) {
    if (user.hasPermission("admin")) {
      deleteAccount(account);
    }
  }
}
```

Good:

```ts
if (!user?.isActive) return;
if (!user.hasPermission("admin")) return;

deleteAccount(account);
```

Bad:

```ts
if ((user.age > 65 && user.yearsOfService > 10) || user.role === "executive") {
  grantFullBenefits(user);
}
```

Good:

```ts
if (user.isEligibleForFullBenefits()) {
  grantFullBenefits(user);
}
```

---

# 12. Duplication and Code Smells

Duplication is the main enemy of clean design.

## Rules

Remove duplicated code.

Remove duplicated knowledge.

Extract repeated concepts into:

* named constants;
* functions;
* classes;
* modules;
* strategies;
* policies;
* domain objects.

Do not abstract too early.

Wait until the repeated concept is clear.

Watch for these smells:

* unclear names;
* misleading names;
* inconsistent vocabulary;
* meaningless distinctions;
* mental mapping;
* long functions;
* functions doing multiple things;
* mixed abstraction levels;
* boolean flag arguments;
* output arguments;
* hidden side effects;
* duplicated logic;
* repeated switch/if-else chains;
* feature envy;
* deep object chains;
* large classes;
* low cohesion;
* primitive obsession;
* null handling spread everywhere;
* swallowed errors;
* commented-out code;
* redundant comments.

Apply the Boy Scout Rule.

Leave the code cleaner than you found it.

When editing existing code, improve nearby naming, structure, or duplication when safe.

Do not perform large unrelated refactors unless requested.

Prefer small, safe improvements.

---

# 13. Avoid Overengineering

Do not add abstraction before there is a clear need.

## Rules

Prefer simple code over speculative flexibility.

Do not introduce these unless they solve a real problem:

* factories;
* strategies;
* managers;
* services;
* interfaces;
* abstract classes;
* generic utilities;
* plugin systems;
* configuration layers;
* inheritance hierarchies.

Do not abstract code just because two lines look similar.

Wait until duplication reveals a stable concept.

Keep local logic local when extracting it would make the code harder to follow.

Avoid generic utilities when a domain-specific function would be clearer.

Bad:

```ts
class UserCreationStrategyFactoryProvider {
  // ...
}
```

Good:

```ts
function createUser(input: CreateUserInput) {
  // ...
}
```

Bad:

```ts
interface UserNameProvider {
  getUserName(): string;
}

class StaticUserNameProvider implements UserNameProvider {
  getUserName() {
    return "Mario";
  }
}
```

Good:

```ts
const userName = "Mario";
```

---

# 14. Security Basics

Do not generate insecure code.

## Rules

Never hardcode:

* secrets;
* tokens;
* passwords;
* API keys;
* private keys;
* credentials.

Never log sensitive data.

Sensitive data includes:

* passwords;
* access tokens;
* refresh tokens;
* session IDs;
* payment data;
* personal identification data;
* private user content.

Validate and sanitize external input.

Use parameterized queries for database access.

Do not build SQL strings by concatenation.

Bad:

```ts
const query = `SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '${email}'`;
```

Good:

```ts
const user = await database.user.findUnique({
  where: { email },
});
```

Do not disable security protections unless explicitly requested and justified.

Do not disable:

* authentication;
* authorization;
* CSRF protection;
* CORS restrictions;
* TLS;
* certificate checks;
* input validation.

Handle authorization in server-side code, not only in the UI.

Bad:

```ts
if (user.role === "admin") {
  showDeleteButton();
}
```

Good:

```ts
if (!currentUser.canDeleteAccount(account)) {
  throw new ForbiddenError();
}

deleteAccount(account);
```

---

# 15. Performance

Write efficient code, but do not optimize prematurely.

## Rules

Prefer clear code first.

Avoid obviously inefficient algorithms on large data.

Avoid repeated database calls inside loops.

Avoid unnecessary network calls.

Avoid unnecessary re-renders in UI code.

Avoid repeated expensive calculations.

Measure before introducing complex optimizations.

Do not sacrifice readability for micro-optimizations.

Bad:

```ts
for (const user of users) {
  const orders = await getOrdersByUserId(user.id);
  sendOrderSummary(user, orders);
}
```

Good:

```ts
const ordersByUserId = await getOrdersGroupedByUserIds(users.map((user) => user.id));

for (const user of users) {
  sendOrderSummary(user, ordersByUserId[user.id] ?? []);
}
```

Avoid caching unless:

* the cost is proven or obvious;
* invalidation is clear;
* stale data is acceptable or handled.

---

# 16. Project Consistency

Follow the existing project style.

## Rules

Respect existing:

* architecture;
* naming conventions;
* formatting;
* folder structure;
* testing style;
* error handling style;
* validation style;
* dependency injection style;
* state management style.

Use existing utilities before creating new ones.

Use existing error types before creating new errors.

Use existing validation patterns before introducing new libraries.

Do not mix paradigms unnecessarily.

Do not introduce a new style in one file if the rest of the module follows another consistent style.

When project conventions conflict with these rules, prefer the project convention unless it is clearly harmful.

---

# 17. AI Refactoring Safety

When refactoring existing code, be conservative.

## Rules

Do not change behavior unless explicitly requested.

Do not rewrite large files unnecessarily.

Do not introduce new architecture unless the current task requires it.

Prefer small, reviewable diffs.

Preserve public APIs unless asked to change them.

Preserve existing naming conventions unless they are clearly harmful.

Preserve error handling and edge cases.

Do not remove code because it looks unused unless confirmed by tests, references, or explicit instruction.

Do not introduce dependencies without justification.

Do not convert working code into abstract patterns just for style.

Explain risky changes before applying them.

When uncertain, choose the least invasive correct change.

Bad:

```ts
// Rewriting an entire service to introduce a new architecture
// when the task only asks to fix one bug.
```

Good:

```ts
// Fix the bug locally.
// Add or update a focused test.
// Improve nearby names only if safe.
```

---

# 18. Practical Refactoring Behavior

When modifying code:

* preserve existing behavior unless explicitly asked to change it;
* prefer the smallest clean change that solves the problem;
* do not introduce unnecessary abstractions;
* do not introduce new dependencies unless justified;
* respect the project’s existing architecture and conventions;
* improve readability without changing public contracts unless required;
* add tests when behavior changes;
* update existing tests when behavior is intentionally changed;
* do not remove edge-case handling unless it is proven obsolete;
* prefer explicit domain concepts over generic utility code;
* keep business logic readable before optimizing;
* avoid broad rewrites;
* avoid unrelated formatting churn;
* avoid drive-by refactors outside the task scope.

---

# 19. Output Expectations

When generating code:

* use meaningful names;
* use pronounceable names;
* use searchable names;
* use consistent vocabulary;
* avoid misleading names;
* avoid cute names;
* avoid magic numbers;
* avoid redundant comments;
* avoid commented-out code;
* keep functions short;
* keep functions focused;
* keep one abstraction level per function;
* avoid boolean flags;
* avoid output arguments;
* avoid hidden side effects;
* avoid long parameter lists;
* avoid null returns;
* avoid null arguments;
* avoid error codes;
* avoid duplicated logic;
* avoid repeated switch/if-else chains;
* avoid deep object chains;
* avoid primitive obsession;
* avoid unsafe types;
* avoid overengineering;
* prefer early returns over nested conditionals;
* prefer readable code over clever code;
* prefer domain-specific names over generic helpers.

When reviewing code:

* identify unclear names;
* identify misleading names;
* identify inconsistent vocabulary;
* identify functions doing too much;
* identify mixed abstraction levels;
* identify side effects;
* identify comments that should become code;
* identify duplication;
* identify repeated conditionals;
* identify feature envy;
* identify Law of Demeter violations;
* identify primitive obsession;
* identify null/error-code problems;
* identify unsafe types;
* identify security risks;
* identify unnecessary abstractions;
* suggest concrete refactorings;
* keep suggestions practical and minimal.

When explaining changes:

* be direct;
* mention behavior changes explicitly;
* mention risk when present;
* mention tests added or needed;
* avoid vague claims like "improved code quality" without saying how.

---

# 20. Non-Negotiables

Do not generate code that:

* uses misleading names;
* uses names that require mental translation;
* uses magic numbers or unexplained constants;
* adds commented-out code;
* adds redundant comments;
* mixes business logic and low-level implementation details in the same function;
* uses boolean flags to switch behavior when two functions would be clearer;
* mutates output arguments when returning a value would be clearer;
* hides side effects behind innocent names;
* returns `null` when an empty collection, Optional/Maybe, Result/Either, Special Case object, or exception is better;
* swallows errors silently;
* introduces duplication;
* creates large classes with unrelated responsibilities;
* repeats switch/if-else logic across the codebase;
* uses unsafe types without justification;
* leaks framework, database, or API details into domain logic;
* hardcodes secrets;
* logs sensitive data;
* disables security checks without explicit justification;
* introduces speculative abstractions;
* performs broad rewrites for small tasks.

---

# Final Principle

Every change should make the code:

* clearer;
* safer;
* easier to test;
* easier to modify.

If a change makes the code more abstract but less understandable, reject it.

If a clever solution needs explanation, prefer a simpler solution.

If the code can explain itself, do not add a comment.

If the rule and the project convention conflict, follow the project convention unless it clearly damages readability, safety, or maintainability.
